# Cursor Rules for humaserverless

## Project Overview
This is a Go library for building serverless HTTP APIs using AWS Lambda and Huma v2. The library provides utilities for converting between AWS Lambda events and HTTP requests/responses.

## Code Style & Conventions

### Go Standards
- Follow standard Go formatting (`go fmt`)
- Use `gofmt` style (tabs for indentation, spaces for alignment)
- Prefer explicit error handling over panics
- Use meaningful variable names
- Keep functions focused and small
- Document exported functions and types with comments

### Naming Conventions
- Use PascalCase for exported types and functions
- Use camelCase for unexported types and functions
- Use descriptive names that explain purpose
- Test functions: `TestFunctionName` with subtests using `t.Run("descriptive-kebab-case-name", ...)`

### Error Handling
- Always handle errors explicitly
- Return errors from functions when operations can fail
- Use `require` from testify for critical test assertions that should stop execution
- Use `assert` from testify for non-critical test assertions

## Testing Standards

### Test Structure
- Use `testify/assert` and `testify/require` packages
- Use `t.Run()` for subtests with descriptive kebab-case names (e.g., "handles-empty-headers")
- Group related tests in the same test function
- Test both success and error cases
- Test edge cases (empty values, nil values, large inputs, etc.)

### Test Naming
- Test functions: `TestFunctionName`
- Subtest names: use kebab-case with descriptive names (e.g., "converts-basic-get-request")
- Be specific about what each test verifies

### Test Coverage
- Aim for comprehensive test coverage
- Test all public functions
- Test error paths
- Test boundary conditions
- Test with realistic AWS Lambda event structures

## AWS Lambda Patterns

### Event Handling
- Always handle `events.APIGatewayV2HTTPRequest` and return `events.APIGatewayV2HTTPResponse`
- Convert Lambda events to standard HTTP requests using `HttpEventToRequest`
- Convert HTTP responses to Lambda events using `ResponseToHttpEvent`
- Handle conversion errors gracefully (return 500 status code)

### Context Usage
- Always accept `context.Context` as the first parameter
- Pass context through to downstream calls
- Use context for cancellation and timeouts when appropriate

## Common Pitfalls to Avoid

### Closure Issues
- **CRITICAL**: When creating closures in loops, always capture loop variables in local variables
- Example of WRONG pattern:
  ```go
  for i := 0; i < len(items); i++ {
      funcs[i] = func() {
          use(items[i])  // ❌ BUG: all closures see the same final value
      }
  }
  ```
- Example of CORRECT pattern:
  ```go
  for i := 0; i < len(items); i++ {
      item := items[i]  // ✅ Capture the value
      funcs[i] = func() {
          use(item)
      }
  }
  ```

### Middleware Patterns
- When chaining middlewares, ensure proper closure handling
- Capture current chain value before reassigning in loops
- Test middleware execution order
- Test middleware that short-circuits (doesn't call next)

### Type Aliases
- Use type aliases for function types (e.g., `HttpHandlerFunc`, `HttpMiddleware`)
- This improves readability and type safety

## Dependencies

### Core Dependencies
- `github.com/aws/aws-lambda-go/events` - AWS Lambda event types
- `github.com/danielgtaylor/huma/v2` - Huma API framework
- `github.com/stretchr/testify` - Testing utilities

### Import Organization
- Standard library imports first
- Third-party imports second
- Local imports last
- Group imports with blank lines between groups

## File Organization

### File Naming
- Use descriptive names that match the primary functionality
- Test files: `*_test.go`
- Keep related functionality in the same file

### Package Structure
- All code in `package humaserverless`
- Export only what needs to be used by consumers
- Keep internal implementation details unexported

## Code Review Checklist

Before submitting code, ensure:
- [ ] All tests pass (`make unit-test`)
- [ ] Code is formatted (`go fmt`)
- [ ] No linter errors (`go vet`)
- [ ] Tests cover new functionality
- [ ] Error cases are handled
- [ ] No closure bugs in loops
- [ ] Context is properly passed through
- [ ] AWS Lambda event structures are handled correctly

## Build & Test Commands

- `make unit-test` - Run all unit tests
- `make tidy` - Tidy go.mod dependencies
- `make lint` - Run go vet and go fmt

## Documentation

- Document exported functions with comments
- Explain complex logic with inline comments
- Keep README.md updated with usage examples
- Document any breaking changes
